thunderbolt-net (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  The driver now learns each forwarded flow's path MTU from the SYN/SYN-ACK
  MSS option and the ICMP Fragmentation Needed / Packet Too Big messages
  that leave the host toward the Thunderbolt peer, and splits that flow's
  aggregates at the learned limit. rx_segment_mtu remains the fallback for
  flows without a record. This is on by default and experimental; set
  rx_segment_learn=0 in /etc/modprobe.d/thunderbolt-net.conf to keep the
  0.3.1 behavior. New ethtool counters: rx_bound_learned_mss,
  rx_bound_learned_icmp, rx_bound_hits.

 -- Shun Li <riverscn@gmail.com>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 04:30:00 +0000

thunderbolt-net (0.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  The packaged default rx_segment_mtu changes from 1500 to 1280. This value
  is the IP length of the segments the host rebuilds from the peer's TCP
  aggregates and must not exceed the smallest MTU on any path beyond the
  host. 1500 stalled uploads from a Mac through the host behind a PPPoE
  uplink (MTU 1492); 1280, the IPv6 minimum, is safe on any path and only
  costs some efficiency on jumbo networks.

  The new value applies on the next module load or reboot. If you copied the
  file to /etc/modprobe.d/thunderbolt-net.conf, your copy keeps 1500; edit it
  if your traffic leaves the host through a smaller path. To raise the value
  again on a network whose every hop carries larger packets, see
  docs/path-mtu.md.

 -- Shun Li <riverscn@gmail.com>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 01:52:06 +0000

thunderbolt-net (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  This version installs /usr/lib/modprobe.d/thunderbolt-net.conf, which
  enables rx_segment=1 rx_segment_mtu=1500 rx_page_pool=1 the next time
  thunderbolt_net is loaded. Earlier versions left these options off unless
  you created opt-in files in /etc/modprobe.d.

  To keep them off, copy the file to /etc/modprobe.d/thunderbolt-net.conf and
  edit the copy before reloading the module or rebooting. A file of the same
  name in /etc replaces the package file; files with other names that sort
  before it do not override it. The old opt-in files
  /etc/modprobe.d/thunderbolt-net-rx.conf and thunderbolt-net-page-pool.conf
  set the same values and can be removed.

 -- Shun Li <riverscn@gmail.com>  Sat, 10 Oct 2026 14:25:29 +0000
